1
00:00:00,580 --> 00:00:04,993
You're handed a job:
move a stone heavier than a car.

2
00:00:05,060 --> 00:00:07,273
No engine. No crane.

3
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:10,600
Every day, for twenty years.

4
00:00:10,940 --> 00:00:13,960
How long would you last?

5
00:00:14,860 --> 00:00:18,473
For centuries, the story was simple:
you wouldn't.

6
00:00:18,540 --> 00:00:21,593
You'd be a slave,
worked until you dropped.

7
00:00:21,660 --> 00:00:23,880
But that story was wrong.

8
00:00:24,290 --> 00:00:28,703
The truth is stranger.
The real builders were paid.

9
00:00:28,770 --> 00:00:31,150
In bread, and in beer.

10
00:00:31,670 --> 00:00:34,563
So what was the job really like?

11
00:00:34,630 --> 00:00:38,163
In 2013, on the shore of the Red Sea,

12
00:00:38,230 --> 00:00:41,563
archaeologists found
something that lets us check.

13
00:00:41,630 --> 00:00:44,650
The diary of a man who did this job.

14
00:00:44,910 --> 00:00:47,043
Written while he was doing it.

15
00:00:47,110 --> 00:00:48,963
His name was Merer.

16
00:00:49,030 --> 00:00:52,203
And for the next ten minutes,
you're on his crew.

17
00:00:52,270 --> 00:00:56,730
Merer was an inspector.
Not a king, not a priest.

18
00:00:56,830 --> 00:01:00,010
A middle manager,
with a crew of men under him.

19
00:01:00,110 --> 00:01:02,323
Dozens of them, maybe more.

20
00:01:02,390 --> 00:01:07,963
And his logbook is the oldest
writing on papyrus ever found anywhere.

21
00:01:08,030 --> 00:01:11,130
It's not poetry. It's a work diary.

22
00:01:11,390 --> 00:01:16,983
Day after day: where the crew slept,
where they loaded, where they sailed.

23
00:01:17,050 --> 00:01:21,270
So, first question.
Who are you on this crew?

24
00:01:21,470 --> 00:01:23,403
You're probably a farmer.

25
00:01:23,470 --> 00:01:27,603
When the Nile floods every summer,
your fields are underwater anyway.

26
00:01:27,670 --> 00:01:31,090
Merer's diary runs from July to November.

27
00:01:31,270 --> 00:01:34,123
That's flood season. The river is high,

28
00:01:34,190 --> 00:01:39,243
the fields are drowned, and the boats
can sail right up to the desert's edge.

29
00:01:39,310 --> 00:01:44,083
So the state calls you up.
You serve your shift, and you go home.

30
00:01:44,150 --> 00:01:45,603
Not a slave.

31
00:01:45,670 --> 00:01:49,723
A worker, on rotation,
with a name on a list.

32
00:01:49,790 --> 00:01:54,250
And remember the pay.
Not coins: coins don't exist yet.

33
00:01:54,510 --> 00:01:57,123
Bread and beer, every day.

34
00:01:57,190 --> 00:02:00,370
And the diary even tells
us who was above you.

35
00:02:00,630 --> 00:02:03,923
Merer reported to a man named Ankhhaf,

36
00:02:03,990 --> 00:02:06,930
the official in charge
of the harbour at Giza.

37
00:02:07,190 --> 00:02:10,443
Ankhhaf was the king's own half-brother.

38
00:02:10,510 --> 00:02:15,083
So the chain ran straight
from your oar to the royal family.

39
00:02:15,150 --> 00:02:18,403
Somebody was counting
every block you moved.

40
00:02:18,470 --> 00:02:22,923
That's day one.
Now let's see if you survive the work.

41
00:02:22,990 --> 00:02:26,883
Merer's job isn't to drag
stones up the pyramid.

42
00:02:26,950 --> 00:02:29,043
His job is to bring them.

43
00:02:29,110 --> 00:02:34,523
The finest white limestone came
from quarries at Tura, across the Nile.

44
00:02:34,590 --> 00:02:37,563
Imagine standing at that cliff face.

45
00:02:37,630 --> 00:02:41,083
The quarrymen cut channels
around each block with copper

46
00:02:41,150 --> 00:02:45,923
chisels and wooden mallets,
then split it free from the rock.

47
00:02:45,990 --> 00:02:48,003
Copper was precious.

48
00:02:48,070 --> 00:02:53,123
A chisel went blunt fast,
and had to be sharpened again and again.

49
00:02:53,190 --> 00:02:57,803
Keep that in mind, because
the copper is how this diary survived.

50
00:02:57,870 --> 00:03:00,843
The diary says what the crew did with it.

51
00:03:00,910 --> 00:03:06,323
They loaded it onto boats, sailed
it across, and unloaded it near Giza.

52
00:03:06,390 --> 00:03:08,003
Picture one trip.

53
00:03:08,070 --> 00:03:11,043
Before dawn
you load the blocks at the quarry,

54
00:03:11,110 --> 00:03:14,443
rope them down to the water,
and lash them on deck.

55
00:03:14,510 --> 00:03:18,970
You sail across while the flood is high
and the current is strong.

56
00:03:19,150 --> 00:03:22,243
You spend the night where
Merer says you stopped.

57
00:03:22,310 --> 00:03:25,283
Next morning
you unload at the king's harbour,

58
00:03:25,350 --> 00:03:27,163
and turn back for more.

59
00:03:27,230 --> 00:03:29,610
About ten round trips a month.

60
00:03:29,710 --> 00:03:33,203
Over five months,
perhaps a thousand blocks.

61
00:03:33,270 --> 00:03:37,523
Which raises a question
every visitor to Giza has asked.

62
00:03:37,590 --> 00:03:39,923
The pyramids stand in the desert.

63
00:03:39,990 --> 00:03:42,963
The Nile today is kilometres away.

64
00:03:43,030 --> 00:03:46,530
So where did Merer's boats actually land?

65
00:03:46,650 --> 00:03:52,263
In 2024, a team using
radar from space found the answer,

66
00:03:52,330 --> 00:03:55,103
buried under sand and fields.

67
00:03:55,170 --> 00:04:00,303
A lost branch of the Nile.
About 64 kilometres long.

68
00:04:00,370 --> 00:04:03,703
31 pyramids were built along its banks.

69
00:04:03,770 --> 00:04:08,863
The causeways of the pyramids run straight
down to where its water used to be.

70
00:04:08,930 --> 00:04:11,423
The river came to the pyramids.

71
00:04:11,490 --> 00:04:14,743
Later it dried up,
and the desert covered it.

72
00:04:14,810 --> 00:04:19,983
So your first answer: you didn't drag
those stones across the desert.

73
00:04:20,050 --> 00:04:21,423
You floated them.

74
00:04:21,490 --> 00:04:25,623
If you'd rather see what the textbooks
got wrong than what they repeat,

75
00:04:25,690 --> 00:04:29,183
that's what this channel does:
one moment in history,

76
00:04:29,250 --> 00:04:31,743
and whether you would have survived it.

77
00:04:31,810 --> 00:04:34,663
Subscribe,
and come along for the whole journey.

78
00:04:34,730 --> 00:04:37,110
Now the part nobody tells you.

79
00:04:37,450 --> 00:04:40,070
What does it feel like to live here?

80
00:04:40,190 --> 00:04:46,003
Just south of the Sphinx, archaeologists
dug up the town where the builders lived.

81
00:04:46,070 --> 00:04:48,563
Barracks. Storerooms.

82
00:04:48,630 --> 00:04:53,483
And bakeries, with rows of heavy
clay pots for baking bread.

83
00:04:53,550 --> 00:04:56,443
Your daily bread came out of those pots.

84
00:04:56,510 --> 00:04:59,123
Your beer came from the same grain.

85
00:04:59,190 --> 00:05:01,810
But here's what surprised the diggers.

86
00:05:01,990 --> 00:05:05,810
The rubbish heaps were full of bones.
Cattle.

87
00:05:05,910 --> 00:05:07,723
Sheep. Goats.

88
00:05:07,790 --> 00:05:12,923
One estimate says the town went through
about eleven cattle and dozens of sheep

89
00:05:12,990 --> 00:05:15,523
and goats every single day.

90
00:05:15,590 --> 00:05:18,083
That's not how you feed slaves.

91
00:05:18,150 --> 00:05:21,803
That's how you feed a workforce
you need to keep strong.

92
00:05:21,870 --> 00:05:24,523
So far,
you're doing better than you thought.

93
00:05:24,590 --> 00:05:27,843
Fed, paid, and home for the harvest.

94
00:05:27,910 --> 00:05:29,970
And you're not just a number.

95
00:05:30,150 --> 00:05:34,203
You belong to a team,
and the teams had names.

96
00:05:34,270 --> 00:05:36,603
Deep inside the Great Pyramid,

97
00:05:36,670 --> 00:05:41,483
in small chambers above the king's
burial room that no visitor was ever

98
00:05:41,550 --> 00:05:46,403
meant to see, the workers left
red paint marks on the stones.

99
00:05:46,470 --> 00:05:50,523
Among them, a team name:
«Friends of Khufu.

100
00:05:50,590 --> 00:05:53,923
At the next pyramid,
built for a later king,

101
00:05:53,990 --> 00:05:59,043
another crew seems to have called
itself «the Drunkards of Menkaure.

102
00:05:59,110 --> 00:06:02,883
Crews with nicknames. Crews competing.

103
00:06:02,950 --> 00:06:07,243
That sounds less like a slave camp,
and more like a building site.

104
00:06:07,310 --> 00:06:09,843
But we haven't touched the stone yet.

105
00:06:09,910 --> 00:06:13,523
But a block on the dock
isn't a block on the pyramid.

106
00:06:13,590 --> 00:06:16,610
It still has to go up. How?

107
00:06:16,730 --> 00:06:20,423
This is the question everyone
types into a search bar,

108
00:06:20,490 --> 00:06:24,943
and here's the honest answer:
experts still argue.

109
00:06:25,010 --> 00:06:26,383
A straight ramp,

110
00:06:26,450 --> 00:06:31,503
a ramp wrapping around the outside,
a ramp hidden inside the pyramid.

111
00:06:31,570 --> 00:06:34,023
Every theory has defenders.

112
00:06:34,090 --> 00:06:37,783
But in 2018 archaeologists found something

113
00:06:37,850 --> 00:06:40,943
real at an old quarry in the desert.

114
00:06:41,010 --> 00:06:44,383
A ramp so steep you'd struggle to walk it,

115
00:06:44,450 --> 00:06:50,743
with stone stairs on both sides, and rows
of holes where wooden posts once stood.

116
00:06:50,810 --> 00:06:53,823
Tie a rope from the sledge
around those posts,

117
00:06:53,890 --> 00:06:59,583
and a team pulling from above can
drag a huge block up a very steep slope.

118
00:06:59,650 --> 00:07:03,310
It dates from about the same time
as the Great Pyramid.

119
00:07:03,570 --> 00:07:07,703
Nobody has proven the builders
used it on the pyramid itself.

120
00:07:07,770 --> 00:07:10,063
But it shows they had the trick.

121
00:07:10,130 --> 00:07:13,023
And on flat ground, there's another trick,

122
00:07:13,090 --> 00:07:16,343
painted on a tomb
wall a few centuries later.

123
00:07:16,410 --> 00:07:21,143
A giant statue on a sledge,
hundreds of men pulling.

124
00:07:21,210 --> 00:07:25,343
And one man at the front,
pouring water onto the sand.

125
00:07:25,410 --> 00:07:28,543
For a long time that looked like a ritual.

126
00:07:28,610 --> 00:07:33,463
In 2014, physicists in Amsterdam
tested it in the lab.

127
00:07:33,530 --> 00:07:37,703
Dry sand piles
up in front of a sledge like a wall.

128
00:07:37,770 --> 00:07:42,383
Wet it just right, and the grains
stick together and stay flat.

129
00:07:42,450 --> 00:07:46,903
The force needed to pull the sledge
can drop by as much as half.

130
00:07:46,970 --> 00:07:49,830
Too much water, and it gets harder again.

131
00:07:50,170 --> 00:07:54,383
Somebody on the crew had to know
exactly how much to pour.

132
00:07:54,450 --> 00:07:57,743
So no aliens, no lost machines.

133
00:07:57,810 --> 00:08:03,543
Ropes, sledges, ramps, water,
and thousands of trained hands.

134
00:08:03,610 --> 00:08:08,023
Around 20,000 people
worked on the pyramid at once.

135
00:08:08,090 --> 00:08:13,103
Most of them moved stone,
cut stone, or hauled it up ramps.

136
00:08:13,170 --> 00:08:16,143
More than two million blocks.

137
00:08:16,210 --> 00:08:18,903
Many weighing about as much as a car.

138
00:08:18,970 --> 00:08:22,383
And their skeletons tell
us what that did to them.

139
00:08:22,450 --> 00:08:25,983
The builders were buried
near the pyramids they built.

140
00:08:26,050 --> 00:08:29,143
Their bones carry the marks of heavy work.

141
00:08:29,210 --> 00:08:32,470
Then the detail that changed the story.

142
00:08:32,810 --> 00:08:37,223
Researchers studied over
2,000 long bones from Giza.

143
00:08:37,290 --> 00:08:40,063
They found broken arms and legs.

144
00:08:40,130 --> 00:08:42,743
But most of them had healed straight.

145
00:08:42,810 --> 00:08:46,983
A broken leg that heals
straight doesn't happen by luck.

146
00:08:47,050 --> 00:08:52,383
Someone set it, splinted it, and kept
that worker fed while he couldn't work.

147
00:08:52,450 --> 00:08:54,343
Archaeologists even describe

148
00:08:54,410 --> 00:08:59,063
a man who lost a limb
and lived for years afterwards.

149
00:08:59,130 --> 00:09:04,103
So if you got hurt on Merer's crew,
you weren't left in the sand.

150
00:09:04,170 --> 00:09:05,823
Somebody came for you.

151
00:09:05,890 --> 00:09:10,783
One question is still open.
Merer worked at Giza.

152
00:09:10,850 --> 00:09:15,290
So why was his diary
found more than a hundred kilometres away,

153
00:09:15,357 --> 00:09:17,550
on the coast of the Red Sea?

154
00:09:17,670 --> 00:09:20,290
Because that's where the copper came from.

155
00:09:20,470 --> 00:09:24,443
Across the water lies Sinai,
with its copper mines.

156
00:09:24,510 --> 00:09:28,723
And on that shore,
the archaeologists found a harbour.

157
00:09:28,790 --> 00:09:35,043
A long stone jetty, about 180 metres,
built out into the open sea.

158
00:09:35,110 --> 00:09:39,003
Probably the oldest
harbour of its kind in the world.

159
00:09:39,070 --> 00:09:41,683
The Egyptians had no forest.

160
00:09:41,750 --> 00:09:44,643
So they built their boats beside the Nile,

161
00:09:44,710 --> 00:09:49,523
took them apart, and carried the pieces
across the desert to this shore.

162
00:09:49,590 --> 00:09:51,883
When the expedition season ended,

163
00:09:51,950 --> 00:09:58,043
they sealed their gear inside caves cut
into the rock, behind huge stone blocks.

164
00:09:58,110 --> 00:10:03,803
And between two of those blocks,
someone stashed a bundle of old papyri.

165
00:10:03,870 --> 00:10:05,763
Merer's diary.

166
00:10:05,830 --> 00:10:10,323
Bone-dry desert, no rain, no visitors.

167
00:10:10,390 --> 00:10:12,963
For four and a half thousand years,

168
00:10:13,030 --> 00:10:17,083
that was the safest
place on Earth to keep a piece of paper.

169
00:10:17,150 --> 00:10:20,163
Now picture what all of this was for.

170
00:10:20,230 --> 00:10:24,883
A single building, about 146 metres tall,

171
00:10:24,950 --> 00:10:27,523
the tallest thing humans had made,

172
00:10:27,590 --> 00:10:31,883
and it would stay the tallest for nearly
four thousand years.

173
00:10:31,950 --> 00:10:35,370
And it isn't just big. It's precise.

174
00:10:35,470 --> 00:10:40,683
Its sides line up with true
north to within a few minutes of a degree.

175
00:10:40,750 --> 00:10:43,403
Less than a tenth of one degree off,

176
00:10:43,470 --> 00:10:46,643
on a base more than two hundred
metres long.

177
00:10:46,710 --> 00:10:48,930
How, without a compass?

178
00:10:49,270 --> 00:10:52,243
One engineer tested a simple idea.

179
00:10:52,310 --> 00:10:55,003
On the day of the autumn equinox, a stick

180
00:10:55,070 --> 00:11:01,163
in the ground casts shadows whose tips
trace an almost perfect east-west line.

181
00:11:01,230 --> 00:11:05,803
Mark the tips through the day,
and the line points east and west.

182
00:11:05,870 --> 00:11:07,963
Square it, and you have north.

183
00:11:08,030 --> 00:11:11,363
A stick, the sun, and patience.

184
00:11:11,430 --> 00:11:15,043
It took around twenty
years of a king's reign.

185
00:11:15,110 --> 00:11:19,483
Year after year,
flood season after flood season.

186
00:11:19,550 --> 00:11:25,083
And on its outside, a skin of polished
white limestone from Tura.

187
00:11:25,150 --> 00:11:28,483
Very likely the stone
Merer's boats were carrying.

188
00:11:28,550 --> 00:11:31,683
His crew was probably
hauling the finishing layer

189
00:11:31,750 --> 00:11:34,683
of the most famous building on Earth.

190
00:11:34,750 --> 00:11:37,283
So let's answer it honestly.

191
00:11:37,350 --> 00:11:39,843
You'd be called up after the flood.

192
00:11:39,910 --> 00:11:44,203
You'd sail stone across a river
that no longer exists.

193
00:11:44,270 --> 00:11:49,603
You'd eat bread, drink beer, and get
more meat than most farmers of your time.

194
00:11:49,670 --> 00:11:54,683
You'd work until your back and knees
showed it, for the rest of your life.

195
00:11:54,750 --> 00:11:59,043
And if the stone broke you,
people would put you back together.

196
00:11:59,110 --> 00:12:02,563
Hard? Brutal, by our standards.

197
00:12:02,630 --> 00:12:05,883
But not the death
camp the old stories promised.

198
00:12:05,950 --> 00:12:09,683
Most of the people who
built the Great Pyramid went home.

199
00:12:09,750 --> 00:12:12,050
And one of them wrote it down.

200
00:12:12,230 --> 00:12:18,403
For four and a half thousand years,
Merer's diary lay in a cave by the sea,

201
00:12:18,470 --> 00:12:20,523
waiting for someone to read it.

202
00:12:20,590 --> 00:12:26,683
Six hundred years before this,
the people of Uruk counted barley on clay.

203
00:12:26,750 --> 00:12:32,090
Here, a crew boss writes a daily
log on paper made from a river plant.

204
00:12:32,350 --> 00:12:37,123
The first cities have become a state
that can plan for twenty years.

205
00:12:37,190 --> 00:12:38,963
Next stop on the journey:

206
00:12:39,030 --> 00:12:44,763
a circle of stones in England, built
by people who left no writing at all.

207
00:12:44,830 --> 00:12:49,050
How do you read a monument
when no one wrote a word?

208
00:12:49,170 --> 00:12:50,990
See you on Wednesday.

